>> title: Boston Baked Beans, Slow Cooker
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: beans, molasses, salt pork, new england, slow cooker, crock pot
>> counters: The Slow Cooker
>> dish: boston-baked-beans
>> kit: Slow Cooker
>> aka: baked beans, Boston beans, molasses baked beans, slow cooker baked beans, crockpot boston baked beans
>> servings: 8
>> time: 9 hr
>> slack: narrow — the parboil is the step with the window: pull the beans before the skins split and the molasses that follows will hold them chalky for the rest of the day, however long you leave the machine on
>> step.2: parboil 30 min, drain
>> step.3: whisk
>> step.4: slow cook on low, 8 hr
>> step.5: reduce on high, lid off, 30 min

A crock is the closest vessel to a bean pot there is, and this is the one bean dish on the shelf where slow beats pressure outright. Two things it still cannot do: it cannot put a skin on the top, and it cannot brown anything — the last half hour with the lid off is the only reduction in the recipe, and it is not the same as an oven's crust. The beans are soaked overnight and boiled hard for half an hour before they go anywhere near the machine. Navy beans are not the dangerous ones, dried red kidney beans are, and there are none in this collection — but the parboil is doing a second job anyway: molasses is sugar and cider vinegar is acid, and both stall a dry bean from ever softening. A bean that goes into the crock still hard stays hard all day.

Simmer @dried navy beans{1%lb}(450 g; soaked overnight) in @water{6%cups}(1.4 L) in a #saucepan{} for ~simmer{30%min}, until the skins split when you blow on a spoonful, then drain.

Whisk @molasses{1/3%cup}(80 mL), @dark brown sugar{1/4%cup}(50 g), @dry mustard{1%tsp}, @cider vinegar{1%Tbs}, @kosher salt{1 1/2%tsp} into @boiling water{2%cups}(475 mL).

Slow cook @&(~2)parboiled beans{} with @&(~1)molasses liquid{}, @salt pork{6%oz}(170 g; rind scored, buried in the middle), @yellow onion{1%medium}(chopped) in a #slow cooker{} on low for ~slow cook{8%hr}, lid on.

Reduce @&(~1)beans{} on high with the lid off for ~reduce{30%min}, until the liquid is a syrup that holds the trail of a spoon for a second. Nothing has evaporated all day, so this is where the sauce comes from.
